P18 = """Your sense of taste is mostly your sense of smell. When you chew food, aromas travel from the back of your mouth up into your nasal cavity, where smell receptors detect them. The tongue itself detects only a handful of basic tastes, such as sweet, salty, sour, bitter and savory (also called umami). The rich differences between, say, a strawberry and a raspberry come mainly from smell.

You can test this with a simple experiment. Hold your nose closed and have someone hand you a jelly bean without telling you the flavor. Chewing, you'll likely notice only that it is sweet. Let go of your nose, and the flavor seems to appear suddenly. The jelly bean didn't change; the aromas simply reached your smell receptors.

This connection explains why food often tastes bland when you have a stuffy nose. It also helps explain a common idea that turns out to be a myth: the \"tongue map,\" which claimed that different parts of the tongue sense different tastes, sweet at the tip, bitter at the back, and so on. Researchers have shown that receptors for all the basic tastes are found across the tongue. The map appears to have come from a misreading of a German study published in 1901, which found only small differences in sensitivity.

Still, the old diagram lived on in textbooks for decades, a reminder that even simple scientific claims deserve a second look."""
Q18 = [
 ("Main idea", "The passage is mainly about:", ["how smell shapes flavor, and why the tongue map is a myth", "how jelly beans are made", "why some foods are bitter", "the history of German science"], "It explains smell's role in flavor and corrects the tongue map."),
 ("Details", "According to the passage, holding your nose while eating a jelly bean makes you likely to notice only that it is:", ["sweet", "sour", "salty", "bitter"], "Paragraph 2 says you'll notice only that it is sweet."),
 ("Details", "According to the passage, the \"tongue map\" is inaccurate because:", ["receptors for all basic tastes are found across the tongue", "the tongue cannot sense sweetness", "smell has no effect on flavor", "taste receptors are found only at the tip"], "Paragraph 3 says so."),
 ("Author's view", "The final sentence suggests the author believes that:", ["widely repeated scientific claims should still be checked", "textbooks are always wrong", "German studies are unreliable", "taste is not worth studying"], "\"Even simple scientific claims deserve a second look.\""),
]

P19 = """The fog came in so fast that Elena lost sight of the shore before she'd finished turning the kayak around. One moment the beach was a pale stripe beyond the water, with her uncle's red truck parked on it; the next there was only white, pressing close on every side, and the soft slap of water against the hull.

She stopped paddling. Her uncle's voice came back to her from that morning, patient and a little bored, as if he'd said it a hundred times: If you can't see, don't guess. Listen.

So she listened. At first there was nothing but her own breathing. Then, faint and to her left, a bell: the buoy at the mouth of the inlet, the one she'd paddled past an hour ago. She knew that the bell was north of the beach. If the bell was on her left, the beach was ahead and slightly right.

She paddled slowly, stopping every twenty strokes to listen again. The bell drifted behind her. Then she heard something better: a car door slamming, and her uncle calling her name, his voice cracking in a way she had never heard before.

When the nose of the kayak scraped sand, he was already in the water to his knees. He didn't say anything about the fog, or about how far out she'd gone. He just held the kayak steady while she climbed out, and then he held on a little longer.""" + FIC
