P21 = """When the city announced it would replace the neighborhood's corner lot, an empty patch of gravel and weeds, with a parking garage, Mrs. Odom wrote a letter. Then she wrote fourteen more. She was eighty-one and had lived on Pine Street for fifty years, and she knew everyone, which meant that by the time the city council held its hearing, the room was full.

People had brought things. A boy held up a jar of tadpoles he had caught in the puddle that formed on the lot every spring. A woman brought a photo of her wedding, taken in front of the lot's lone magnolia tree. Mr. Hale, who ran the hardware store, brought a hand-drawn map showing where a garden could go, and where benches might sit in the shade.

The council members listened politely. They explained that the city needed parking. Then a councilwoman asked how many people in the room had driven there. Three hands went up. Everyone else had walked.

The garage was never built. It took four years, two more hearings and a great many bake sales, but the corner lot became Odom Park. There is a bench under the magnolia now, and every spring, children still catch tadpoles in a shallow pond that the city, after some argument, agreed to leave alone.""" + FIC
